    "id": "2097625001948013023",
    "text": "U.S. FUEL PRICES have risen to their highest level since the war with Iran began as the renewed rise in crude prices is compounded by crack spreads still at near-record highs.  The combined cost of fuels and refining margins is now higher than it was in early April before the first of the ceasefires. So far this month the combined cost of U.S. crude ($92) and the 3-2-1 crack ($62) is higher than in May ($98 and $53) and April ($99 and $47). The intense upward pressure on fuel prices at wholesale level is putting upward pressure on prices at the pump, increasing costs for households and businesses, and playing into discontent about higher prices and the cost of living:",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-09T09:55:47+00:00",
    "tweetTime": 1788947747000,
